(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-1-pharm-drill-cholinergic"]={"title":"Cholinergic & Anticholinergic Drill","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which neuromuscular blocker causes malignant hyperthermia?","choices":["Succinylcholine","Rocuronium","Vecuronium","Atracurium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Malignant hyperthermia and hyperkalaemia. Treatment is rapid cooling and dantrolene. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which neuromuscular blocker releases histamine?","choices":["Succinylcholine","Atracurium","Rocuronium","Pancuronium"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Histamine release is what separates it from the other nondepolarizing blockers. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Which drug reactivates acetylcholinesterase after organophosphate poisoning?","choices":["Atropine","Physostigmine","Pralidoxime","Neostigmine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It reactivates inhibited acetylcholinesterase. Two limits: it does not enter the central nervous system, and it cannot overcome reversible inhibitors such as physostigmine. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which cholinesterase inhibitor is used to DIAGNOSE myasthenia gravis?","choices":["Pyridostigmine","Neostigmine","Physostigmine","Edrophonium"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Very short acting, so it is used for diagnosis and to reverse nondepolarizing blockers after surgery. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which cholinesterase inhibitor is used for the CHRONIC management of myasthenia gravis?","choices":["Pyridostigmine","Edrophonium","Physostigmine","Pralidoxime"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Long acting, so it is the maintenance drug — edrophonium is the diagnostic one. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which agent stimulates a postpartum or postoperative atonic bladder and has NO nicotinic activity?","choices":["Carbachol","Bethanechol","Pilocarpine","Neostigmine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Muscarinic only, with no nicotinic action, so it is used for the atonic bladder. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which cholinergic agonist has both muscarinic AND nicotinic activity and is used to lower intraocular pressure?","choices":["Bethanechol","Pilocarpine","Carbachol","Atropine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Both receptor types, and it decreases intraocular pressure in glaucoma. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which drug lowers intraocular pressure AND stimulates salivation in dry mouth?","choices":["Carbachol","Bethanechol","Timolol","Pilocarpine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Both indications belong to it — glaucoma and xerostomia. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Dry mouth, blurred vision and “sandy eyes” describe the effects of which drug?","choices":["Atropine","Bethanechol","Pilocarpine","Neostigmine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Classic antimuscarinic effects, along with urinary retention and constipation. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"“Mad as a hatter, blind as a bat, red as a beet” describes toxicity from which group?","choices":["Cholinergic agonists","Antimuscarinics","Beta blockers","Organophosphates"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The anticholinergic toxidrome. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which drug shows bradycardia at lower doses but tachycardia at higher doses?","choices":["Propranolol","Bethanechol","Atropine","Isoproterenol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The dose-dependent reversal that catches people out. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which antimuscarinic is used to prevent motion sickness, with instructions to wash the hands after applying it?","choices":["Atropine","Ipratropium","Oxybutynin","Scopolamine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A patch for motion sickness — and hands must be washed, or the drug is transferred to the eye. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which inhaled antimuscarinics are used for maintenance bronchodilation in chronic obstructive pulmonary disease?","choices":["Tiotropium","Albuterol","Scopolamine","Atropine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Maintenance inhaled bronchodilators; glycopyrrolate in the same group is also used for drooling and hyperhidrosis. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which antimuscarinics lower intravesical pressure and increase bladder capacity?","choices":["Bethanechol","Oxybutynin","Ipratropium","Scopolamine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The bladder antimuscarinics, reducing the frequency of bladder contractions. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which cholinesterase inhibitor crosses into the central nervous system and can cause convulsions at high dose?","choices":["Neostigmine","Pyridostigmine","Physostigmine","Pralidoxime"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It enters the central nervous system, which is why it is the one used for anticholinergic toxicity — and why it convulses at high dose. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which drugs slow the progression of Alzheimer's disease and mostly cause gastrointestinal distress?","choices":["Neostigmine","Edrophonium","Atropine","Donepezil"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The centrally acting cholinesterase inhibitors, whose limiting side effect is gastrointestinal. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Salivation, flushing, falling blood pressure, abdominal pain, diarrhoea and bronchospasm together are the effects of what?","choices":["Cholinergic excess","Anticholinergic excess","Beta blockade","Alpha-1 blockade"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The muscarinic picture produced by cholinesterase inhibitors and direct agonists. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which class must be used with caution in reactive airway disease because it causes bronchospasm?","choices":["Antimuscarinics","Cholinergic agonists","Beta-2 agonists","Alpha-1 blockers"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bronchospasm is the reason for caution in reactive airway disease. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Organophosphates are most commonly encountered as what?","choices":["Prescription eye drops","Topical acne treatments","Agricultural insecticides","Inhaled anaesthetics"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Irreversible cholinesterase inhibitors used as insecticides; poisoning shows nicotinic or muscarinic signs. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which drug is given as an ANTIDOTE to competitive neuromuscular blockers after surgery?","choices":["Succinylcholine","Atropine","Pralidoxime","Neostigmine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Raises acetylcholine to out-compete the blocker at the junction. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Nondepolarizing blockers are used for which purpose?","choices":["Relaxing skeletal muscle for intubation","Treating myasthenia gravis","Reversing organophosphate poisoning","Lowering intraocular pressure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Adjuvants in anaesthesia — intubation, fracture alignment and dislocation correction. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Nicotine at higher doses does what to blood pressure and to gut and bladder activity?","choices":["Blood pressure rises further and gut activity increases","Blood pressure falls and gut activity ceases","Both are unchanged","Blood pressure rises while gut activity ceases"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Stimulation first, then paralysis of all ganglia — so the early effects reverse. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"Hyperkalaemia after a neuromuscular blocker points to which agent?","choices":["Rocuronium","Cisatracurium","Succinylcholine","Pancuronium"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hyperkalaemia alongside malignant hyperthermia — the two dangers of the depolarizing blocker. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Malignant hyperthermia is treated with rapid cooling and which drug?","choices":["Pralidoxime","Neostigmine","Atropine","Dantrolene"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Dantrolene, with rapid cooling. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which agent has NO therapeutic use but causes a serious pressor reaction in a patient on a monoamine oxidase inhibitor?","choices":["Tyramine","Nicotine","Cocaine","Ephedrine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. No therapeutic use; found in fermented foods such as cheese and wine. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"Which cholinergic agent is used to decrease intraocular pressure in glaucoma AND has both receptor actions, given intraocularly during surgery?","choices":["Bethanechol","Carbachol","Physostigmine","Scopolamine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both muscarinic and nicotinic, used intraocularly. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which effects would you expect from a patient who has taken too much of an antimuscarinic?","choices":["Salivation, diarrhoea and bronchospasm","Bradycardia and hypotension","Confusion, hallucinations and delirium","Tendon rupture and photosensitivity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The central antimuscarinic picture, on top of dry mouth, blurred vision and retention. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which of these groups inhibits cholinesterase IRREVERSIBLY?","choices":["Cholinergic agonists","Antimuscarinics","Nondepolarizing blockers","Organophosphates"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Irreversible, which is why the enzyme has to be reactivated with pralidoxime rather than simply out-competed. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which antimuscarinic is used for excessive drooling in cerebral palsy and for hyperhidrosis?","choices":["Glycopyrrolate","Scopolamine","Oxybutynin","Atropine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Named for both, alongside its inhaled use in the same family. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Why can pralidoxime not be used against physostigmine poisoning?","choices":["It is too slow to act","It cannot overcome reversible inhibitors","It is inactivated by the liver first","It only works in children"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It reactivates the enzyme after IRREVERSIBLE inhibition; a reversible inhibitor is not bound the same way. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 42"}]};
